    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f4b10e7_ed24fef3",
        "filename": "core/java/android/os/SystemProperties.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 263,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-14T00:36:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We may need to change it in the future, more likely adding than deleting.\n\npublic native bool isVendorProp?\n\nUsually, we are distinguishing vendor/system props by SELinux, but this is impossible for apps. It\u0027s really messy I feel even to let an app process get ahold of these properties, because then we aren\u0027t sure from an SELinux perspective if the platform code or the app is accessing them. Another example, do we imagine non-vendor system apps calling this API? I wonder if the restriction should be done inside of \u0027set\u0027 using PM isVendor. Although, yeah, this is a really complicated route to go down. Impossible to get everything right.\n\nYeah, actually, aren\u0027t there Treble violations here? Similar to b/120188076. Any vendor app can use system properties?",
      "parentUuid": "1d333803_5266929c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 244,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 263,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "3e59d61109bf8917c2d10954af403ee8a3d63b16",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4021daba_be368081",
        "filename": "core/java/android/os/SystemProperties.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 263,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-16T09:13:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What I wanted to do here was to allow vendor codes to access vendor\u0027s properties with no hacking or workaround. Can\u0027t vendor assign their own SELinux contexts to their own apps with seapp_contexts? If so, vendors will be able to control accessibilities of vendor properties so platform code or apps other than vendor can\u0027t access vendor\u0027s properties. Non-vendor system apps will get nothing with this method, because vendor\u0027s sepolicy rules won\u0027t allow non-vendor apps to access the properties.\n\nFor using PM isVendor, I don\u0027t want \u0027set\u0027 to be @SystemApi or public API. For Treble violation, vendor or odm properties are owned by vendor, so I can\u0027t see any violation.\n\nBTW if distinguishing vendor/system apps is indeed impossible, this CL has no meaning. Is it?",
      "parentUuid": "9f4b10e7_ed24fef3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 244,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 263,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "3e59d61109bf8917c2d10954af403ee8a3d63b16",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "10810a5a_9c375d91",
        "filename": "core/java/android/os/SystemProperties.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 263,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-16T19:53:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "PM does have an \u0027isVendor\u0027 function for apks internally indicating where it was loaded from, but it gets complicated when you start talking about multiple apps being in the same process.\n\nidk, I am very curious what API council thinks here. Because of all the bad interactions possible between system/vendor apps, I don\u0027t really like Java on vendor very much. I think we may want to lean more towards fixing the architecture by disallowing non-unbundled apps here rather than adding hacks for all these specific cases. It\u0027s very tricky to get correctness for these types of things.",
      "parentUuid": "4021daba_be368081",
